# Futurologism-0 of American Ethnicities [C-0]

- **Futurologism-0 of Hispanic/Latino America:** aging of the U.S.-born majority within the group, language shift across generations, regional diversity (Mexican, Puerto Rican, Cuban, Central and South American origins), and a politically unpredictable electorate.
- **Futurologism-0 of Black America:** the growing African and Caribbean immigrant share, Great Migration reversal toward the South, wealth-gap trajectories, and how "African American" vs. "Black" identity categories evolve.
- **Futurologism-0 of Asian America:** fastest-growing electorate, extreme internal diversity (South, East, Southeast Asian), shifting immigration streams, and whether the "Asian" label survives as a coherent category.
- **Futurologism-0 of White America:** a projected loss of majority share, whether "white" stays a single category, rural/urban divergence, and the identity politics of becoming one group among many.
- **Futurologism-0 of Indigenous America (AI/AN):** nation sovereignty and land return, language revitalization, tribal enrollment rules under demographic decline, and census undercount.
- **Futurologism-0 of Middle Eastern & North African (MENA) America:** a newly counted category as of 2024, first reliable data, civil-rights visibility, and gaps between how people self-identify and how they're classified.
- **Futurologism-0 of Native Hawaiian & Pacific Islander America:** displacement from home islands (housing costs, climate), diaspora growth on the mainland, and visibility once data is separated from "Asian."
- **Futurologism-0 of Multiracial & Mixed America:** the fastest-growing group, how intermarriage reshapes every category, and whether categories dissolve or multiply.

## Cross-cutting forces
- Intermarriage and ancestry testing blurring lines
- Census category redesign changing who is counted
- Climate migration and regional realignment
- AI and language tech shaping heritage languages
- Generational and political shifts within every group

